            <ArticleTitle>Expression of neutrophil and monocyte CD11B/CD18 adhesion molecules at different sites of the coronary tree in unstable angina pectoris.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>564-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>To assess the site of leukocyte activation in unstable angina, the expression of neutrophil and monocyte CD11B/CD18 adhesion molecules in 26 patients was measured from blood samples taken from the coronary ostium, the coronary sinus, and the coronary artery just distal to the culprit lesion (postobstructive chamber). CD11B/CD18 adhesion molecules detected by direct immunofluorescence evaluated by flow cytometry were significantly higher in the coronary sinus blood than in both the coronary ostium and the postobstructive chamber blood, suggesting that leukocyte activation takes place at the microcirculatory interface with the injured myocardium, probably as the result of short but repeated episodes of myocardial ischemia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
